<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Satsuma Domain</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Satsuma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Satsuma Domain was a powerful feudal domain in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, ruled by the Shimazu family. During the late Tokugawa period it acquired Western weapons and military training. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> note that Satsuma, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Choshu</a>, led the opposition that overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> and brought about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain.jpg" alt="Satsuma Domain" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mass Ave 975 at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kagoshimajo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Satsuma Domain was a han of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> from 1600 to 1871. It was based at Kagoshima Castle in Satsuma Province, in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, and covered territory in the provinces of Satsuma, Osumi and Hyuga. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-clan">Shimazu clan</a>, who had ruled the Kagoshima area since the 1200s, governed it throughout as tozama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600 they fought on the losing side, yet they remained among the most powerful clans in the archipelago. The domain's assessed value peaked at 770,000 koku, the second highest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> after the Kaga Domain.</p><p>In 1609 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-iehisa">Shimazu Iehisa</a> asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> for permission to invade the Ryukyu Kingdom. After a three-month war against stiff resistance, Satsuma captured the capital Shuri and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-nei">Sho Nei</a>. In the peace that followed it annexed the Amami and Tokara Islands, demanded tribute and made the king pledge loyalty to its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Satsuma's wealth came from its size and productivity, from its great distance from Edo and from its hold over Ryukyu, whose tributary status with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> let it trade in Chinese goods when strict maritime prohibitions began in the 1630s. The Shimazu won exceptions from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>: they were allowed more than one castle, giving castles to their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and running the domain somewhat like a mini-shogunate, and they travelled to Edo for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sankin-kotai">sankin-kotai</a> only once every two years. The ban on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was enforced there perhaps more strictly and brutally than anywhere else in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p>The killing of an Englishman by Satsuma retainers in the Namamugi Incident of 1862 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>'s bombardment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a> in 1863. Satsuma withstood the attack, but it showed the need to import Western technology. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> moved to defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Choshu</a> in a second expedition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigo Takamori</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okubo-toshimichi">Okubo Toshimichi</a> switched sides, and the Satcho Alliance with Choshu was brokered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakamoto-ryoma">Sakamoto Ryoma</a> of Tosa. The expedition ended in disaster for the shogunate, which was defeated again at Toba-Fushimi in 1868.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> ended in 1869. In 1871 the Meiji government abolished the domains, and Kagoshima-han became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima-prefecture">Kagoshima Prefecture</a>. The new government was largely dominated by politicians from Satsuma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Choshu</a>, and figures from the two areas dominated Japanese government until roughly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>, discontented as their authority withered, rose under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigo Takamori</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-rebellion">Satsuma Rebellion</a> of 1877.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Satsuma Domain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>These forces proved no match for <b>Satsuma</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Further contacts with Korea, the Ryukyu Kingdom and the Ainu were channelled through the domains of Tsushima, <b>Satsuma</b> and Matsumae.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1865 Takasugi Shinsaku and the Kiheitai militia ousted Chōshū’s conservatives, and on 7 March 1866 Sakamoto Ryōma brought Kido Takayoshi and Saigō Takamori together in the <b>Satsuma</b>–Chōshū Alliance.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Officers of the police brigade, many of them of samurai origin from <b>Satsuma</b> itself, proposed to the army commander Yamagata Aritomo that the best swordsmen of the police be sent in; Yamagata, the champion of conscription, agreed reluctantly and named the unit himself.</blockquote><a href="/songs/battotai">Battōtai</a></li><li><blockquote>Its opening words were changed in the Kamakura period, when samurai sang it as a festive song; in the Edo period it was popular among the people, and was sung as a New Year song in the Ōoku of Edo Castle and in the <b>Satsuma</b> domain.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kimigayo">Kimigayo</a></li><li><blockquote>Forces from <b>Satsuma</b> and Choshu seized the imperial palace in Kyoto and an imperial decree abolished the Tokugawa shogunate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-3">3 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
